

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next, and this is the one that decides how much of the module there is.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much of a form's job the shared piece owns. Today [38;5;153mapp.js[39m does three things inline — read the inputs,
  check them, report the failure to the console. Reuse could cover just the middle one, or all three. The more it owns,
  the less each new form writes, but the more each new form has to look like the login form.

  - [1mRules only[0m — a [38;5;153mvalidate(values, rules)[39m that takes a plain object and returns per-field errors. Each form still reads
    its own inputs and displays its own errors. Buys: no DOM assumptions, trivially testable, works for a form of any
    shape. Costs: every new form repeats ~10 lines of read-and-display glue.
  - [1mRules + form binding[0m — the above, plus a helper that reads values off a [38;5;153m<form>[39m element by input [38;5;153mname[39m and runs the
    rules. Buys: kills the repeated read glue; display stays the form's business. Costs: forms must name their inputs
    (login's currently only have [38;5;153mid[39m), so this touches [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.
  - [1mRules + binding + error rendering[0m — also writes messages into the DOM next to each field and handles the submit
    wiring. Buys: a new form is a rule object and a markup convention. Costs: locks every form into one markup and one
    error-display style; this is the part that's painful to back out of once forms depend on it.

  [1mMy recommendation: rules + form binding.[0m With one real consumer, the rendering layer would be designed against a
  single guess about what error UI should look like — that's the piece I'd rather see two forms before committing to.
  Rules + binding removes the genuinely repetitive part and leaves display free.
